 "final_text": "# Final answer\n\n## What I changed\n\nOnly the last layer: `fc.weight` and `fc.bias`. No other tensor is edited.\n\nThe new decision direction (row 1 minus row 0) is built in closed form from the four group means of the 2,048 avgpool features over the practice set:\n\n- `d_bird` = waterbird minus landbird mean, averaged over the two backgrounds.\n- `d_bg` = water minus land mean, averaged over the two bird classes.\n- `v = d_bird \u2212 (d_bird\u00b7d_bg / d_bg\u00b7d_bg)\u00b7d_bg`, rescaled so that bird identity is worth 16 logits, as in the original model.\n- Bias: the midpoint between the two class means along `v`, plus 0.25 logits toward \"waterbird\".\n\nThe common mode of the two fc rows is left untouched. By construction the background mean shift along `v` is exactly zero.\n\n## Results\n\nAll 50 forward passes are used. The final pass verified the submitted tensors on the full practice set:\n\n| | LL | LW | WL | WW | worst | mean |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Original | 99.4 | 82.6 | 72.9 | 96.2 | 72.9 | 97.7 |\n| Submitted edit (practice, in-sample) | 96.8 | 91.9 | 94.0 | 94.0 | 91.9 | 95.95 |\n| Held-out, centre crop (offset 0) | 97.4 | 92.3 | 92.5 | 93.2 | 92.3 | 96.25 |\n| Held-out, mirrored (offset 0) | 96.8 | 92.9 | 92.5 | 94.0 | 92.5 | 96.0 |\n| Held-out, shifted crop (offset 0) | 96.8 | 92.7 | 92.5 | 93.2 | 92.5 | 95.8 |\n\nThe held-out rows were run with the bias at the midpoint, with each image scored by an edit built from the other half of the practice set. With the submitted +0.25 offset, the view-averaged held-out figures are about 96.8 / 91.9 / 93.0 / 93.5.\n\nPaired against the original on the practice set, the submitted edit fixes 28 waterbird/land images and breaks none, and fixes 46 landbird/water images and breaks 3. It costs 12 landbird/land and 3 waterbird/water images.\n\nI expect roughly worst group 92% and mean 96% on the test set. My rough estimate of meeting both targets is about 0.9; the main risk is sampling noise in the two 642-image waterbird groups.\n\n## What goes wrong in the network\n\n- **The last layer reads the background.** Switching land to water moves the margin by 8.1 logits, half the bird's own 16. The original direction is essentially `0.114\u00b7d_bird + 0.068\u00b7d_bg` (84% of its squared norm lies in that plane).\n- **The background contribution is diffuse.** The top 10 channels carry 12% of it and it takes about 800 for 95%, so deleting channels cannot work; a direction-level edit is needed.\n- **The background is linearly readable from layer1 on; bird evidence is built in layer4.** The two directions are orthogonal at the end of layer3 (cosine 0.04) and become aligned in layer4.1 and layer4.2 (0.30, then 0.57).\n- **The last block fuses them.** layer4.2 writes 59% of the background shift and 87% of the bird shift that fc reads.\n- **One direction at the end of layer3 feeds the shortcut.** Hiding it from layer4.0 removes 84% of fc's background shift. Injecting \u00b11 unit of it (two running-mean tensors only) steers waterbird/land accuracy from 38% to 94%.\n- **Bird and scene enter layer4.2 as separable signals.** Blinding its first convolution to the scene direction gives a bird classifier with fc untouched (worst group 91.0% at strength 1.5). Blinding it to the bird direction gives a background classifier (7\u201310% on the mismatched groups). A same-size control direction does nothing.\n\n## Evidence that the edit acts on that mechanism\n\n- **Dose-response in the real network.** Worst group is 84.8 / 88.0 / 92.7 / 88.2 for cancellation strength 0 / 0.5 / 1 / 1.5 (bias at the midpoint). Projecting out a random direction with the same overlap leaves it at 84.4.\n- **Composite images.** Swapping only the surroundings moves the original's margin by 6.2\u20138.3 logits and the edited model's by \u22120.9 to +0.15. Swapping only the bird moves both by 15.0. Pooled accuracy over three composite sets goes from 95.8 / 83.2 / 77.4 / 94.7 to 91.6 / 92.6 / 93.2 / 93.2.\n- **Bird removed.** The original calls 92% of water scenes and 14% of land scenes \"waterbird\"; the edited model calls 61% and 53%.\n- **Per-position evidence (96 random images).** The scene vote falls from 7.6 to 0.35 logits while the bird vote is essentially unchanged (17.6 to 16.8).\n\n## What I tried and did not keep\n\n- **Fitted last layers.** Logistic and shrunk-Fisher refits with 2,048 free weights held out at worst group about 90%, and worse when weakly regularised. In a nested selection the closed-form direction was chosen in 10 of 12 splits.\n- **Upstream edits as part of the repair.** Blinding layer4.1, layer4.2, or the layer3 or layer2 scene direction, with fc re-derived, gave no gain that generalised. The layer2 cut helped four practice waterbird/land images in a dose-dependent way but failed two independent composite checks (4 fixed, 7 broken), so I rejected it.\n- **Bias offset.** Estimated pass probability is flat for offsets from 0 to +0.5; I chose +0.25 as the centre of that plateau. Offset 0 scores 97.6 / 92.7 / 93.2 / 94.0 in-sample and is saved as `work/best_1`.\n\n## Limits\n\nAbout 7\u20138% of each hard group remains wrong. These are mostly bird-evidence failures that no background fix addresses:\n\n- Species labelled \"waterbird\" that look like landbirds: all 8 pewee-like practice images are wrong, and the features cannot separate them from flycatchers.\n- Tiny or oddly posed birds.\n- Water photos that contain real ducks.\n\nThe submitted tensors are in `work/edited` (identical to `work/best_2`); `work/notes.md` logs every pass."
